WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.680
Which phase is each cell in?

2
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:07.540
Freeze this dish of thousands: every cell looks unlabelled, but each carries clues.

3
00:00:08.733 --> 00:00:12.293
In chapter 1, one cell's kinase rose and fell once.

4
00:00:13.593 --> 00:00:15.113
Its genome was copied once.

5
00:00:16.353 --> 00:00:19.713
So DNA doubles once per cycle, then halves at division.

6
00:00:23.617 --> 00:00:25.357
Start with DNA content.

7
00:00:26.817 --> 00:00:28.997
One C is one haploid genome's DNA.

8
00:00:30.337 --> 00:00:35.137
A diploid cell holds 2C in G1, and 4C in G2 and mitosis.

9
00:00:36.567 --> 00:00:38.887
The cell has not become tetraploid.

10
00:00:40.087 --> 00:00:42.527
It still has two homologous sets of chromosomes.

11
00:00:44.007 --> 00:00:49.587
By G2, each chromosome has two sister chromatids, doubling DNA without changing

12
00:00:49.587 --> 00:00:54.327
ploidy. C measures DNA amount, not chromosome-set number.

13
00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:03.830
Stain fixed cells with propidium iodide, a dye that fluoresces when bound to nucleic

14
00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:08.290
acid. Add RNase, because the dye also binds RNA.

15
00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.170
A flow cytometer reads each cell's fluorescence.

16
00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:19.030
After RNA is removed, fluorescence is a proxy for DNA amount.

17
00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:25.910
Under the same staining conditions, a 4C cell gives about twice a 2C cell's signal.

18
00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:30.490
The comparison needs single cells measured consistently.

19
00:01:33.183 --> 00:01:35.283
The dots pile up into a histogram.

20
00:01:36.463 --> 00:01:39.283
There is a peak at 2C and another at 4C.

21
00:01:40.663 --> 00:01:43.303
Cells in S spread between them, partway through copying.

22
00:01:44.433 --> 00:01:46.493
Read the axes before the labels.

23
00:01:47.973 --> 00:01:52.273
Horizontal position is fluorescence per cell, used here for DNA amount.

24
00:01:53.753 --> 00:01:55.153
Bar height is the number of cells.

25
00:01:56.413 --> 00:02:00.593
This plot is a population snapshot, not one cell moving through time.

26
00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:03.987
Here is one cell from the 4C peak.

27
00:02:05.207 --> 00:02:06.607
It has copied all its DNA.

28
00:02:08.007 --> 00:02:10.567
Is it still in G2, or already in mitosis?

29
00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:18.420
The histogram cannot say.

30
00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:22.540
Mitotic cells share the 4C peak with G2.

31
00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.420
Quiescent G0 cells share the 2C peak with G1.

32
00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:33.300
DNA content narrows the phase; other markers can separate some look-alikes.

33
00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:35.810
Two more stains help.

34
00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:39.030
EdU is a thymidine look-alike.

35
00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:45.290
During a short pulse, cells copying DNA build it in, and click chemistry adds a

36
00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:48.410
glowing tag. It marks cells in S.

37
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.220
EdU also catches cells at the borders.

38
00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:57.940
Early S cells can sit near the 2C peak, and late S cells near 4C.

39
00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:04.900
DNA content can blur both into neighbouring phases; EdU reveals ongoing synthesis.

40
00:03:06.083 --> 00:03:10.683
An antibody detects histone H3 phosphorylated on serine 10.

41
00:03:11.963 --> 00:03:15.123
This mark spreads across chromosomes as they condense.

42
00:03:16.263 --> 00:03:19.943
It helps find mitotic cells, but it is not an exact boundary.

43
00:03:21.083 --> 00:03:25.643
The signal begins near centromeres in late G2, before mitosis.

44
00:03:27.003 --> 00:03:31.683
It spreads by prophase, remains strong through metaphase, then fades during

45
00:03:31.683 --> 00:03:37.743
anaphase. So a positive cell is usually mitotic, but timing keeps the call

46
00:03:37.743 --> 00:03:38.743
approximate.

47
00:03:41.367 --> 00:03:45.167
G0 is harder: no single stain marks it cleanly.

48
00:03:46.307 --> 00:03:49.127
Together, these measures classify most cycling cells.

49
00:03:50.327 --> 00:03:55.487
But G0 versus G1 and the G2-mitosis boundary remain imperfect.

50
00:03:56.717 --> 00:03:58.577
So which phase is each cell in?

51
00:03:59.657 --> 00:04:04.897
DNA amount narrows the choices; EdU and phospho-H3 sharpen the answer.

52
00:04:06.277 --> 00:04:07.717
G0 remains unresolved.

53
00:04:09.237 --> 00:04:12.197
How long each phase lasts is the text's next question.
